function renderComparisonPreview(){
 const host=$('#comparison-panel');if(!host)return;
 try{const d=comparisonSettings(state.params,state.reference,state.paths,state.shocks),last=d.rates.at(-1),warning=d.lowerSteps?'The scenario adopts a smaller fraction of the same knowledge gap in '+d.lowerSteps+' step(s). A preset name does not override your reference.':!d.isolatedAdoptionComparison?'Other settings or interventions also differ; the full result cannot be attributed only to diffusion.':'Starting settings match apart from adoption controls. The full model still includes income, investment and finance feedback.';
 host.innerHTML=`<div class="eyebrow">NEXT RUN · CHECK THE COMPARISON</div><div class="section-header"><h2>What are you actually comparing?</h2><button class="text-button" id="align-reference">Match reference to these starting settings</button></div><p class="small">Configured domestic-learning rate before workforce-skills effects at the final step: <strong>reference ${fmt(100*last.reference,2)}%</strong> · <strong>scenario ${fmt(100*last.scenario,2)}%</strong>. This combines diffusion, absorptive capacity and research capability. The simulated workforce-skills multiplier changes the actual learning response.</p><p class="comparison-warning">${esc(warning)}</p><details><summary>${d.differences.length} initial-setting differences · ${state.paths.length} parameter paths · ${state.shocks.length} shocks</summary>${d.differences.length?`<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Reference</th><th>Scenario</th></tr></thead><tbody>${d.differences.map(x=>`<tr><td>${esc(x.label)}</td><td>${fmt(x.reference,4)}</td><td>${fmt(x.scenario,4)}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>`:'<p class="small">All initial economic parameters match. Timelines and shocks may still differ.</p>'}</details>`;
 }catch{host.textContent='Complete the parameter and timeline fields to inspect the comparison.';}
}
function renderMechanismDiagnostics(){
 if(!result)return;const r=result,d=r.diagnostics,f=d.firstSeed,last=r.params.periods,paired=r.paired;
 const perCapitaGrowth=quantile(paired.map(x=>x.realGDPPerCapitaGrowth),.5),tech=quantile(paired.map(x=>x.technology),.5),gdp=quantile(paired.map(x=>x.output),.5),local=d.localDirectEffect.at(-1);
 let index=paired.findIndex(x=>x.output<0);if(index<0)index=0;const b=r.raw.baseline[index].rows[last],s=r.raw.scenario[index].rows[last],seed=r.raw.baseline[index].seed;
 const contributions=[['Household consumption','consumption'],['Public purchases','government'],['Fixed investment','investment'],['Inventory change','inventoryChange']].map(([label,key])=>({label,value:s[key]-b[key]}));
 contributions.push({label:'Net exports',value:(s.exports-s.imports)-(b.exports-b.imports)});
 $('#mechanism-diagnostics').innerHTML=`<div class="eyebrow">DISPLAYED RUN · ${esc(r.context.country)} · PERIOD ${last}</div><h2>Learning, firm weights and GDP are different outcomes.</h2><div class="diagnostic-grid"><div><span>Real GDP per capita growth</span><strong>${signed(perCapitaGrowth)} pp</strong><small>Final-year annual growth: scenario minus reference. Accounts for population change.</small></div><div><span>Technology · current capital weights</span><strong>${signed(tech)}%</strong><small>Includes surviving firms, entry, exit and changing sizes.</small></div><div><span>Model GDP</span><strong>${signed(gdp)}%</strong><small>Conditional on unestimated macro rules.</small></div></div><p class="small">Numbers above are median paired differences from the reference, not changes from the previous period. A negative gap can mean slower growth than the reference even when both paths rise.</p>
 <details><summary>Separate the direct learning effect from feedback</summary><p>At identical reference states, changing only the effective adoption rate gives a median last-step knowledge change of <strong>${signed(local.median,5)} index units</strong>. If adoption is faster, this local term cannot be negative: a firm moves toward better available knowledge and never subtracts what it already knows. This is a tested model property, not an empirical causal estimate.</p>${f?`<p>For displayed seed ${f.seed}, the exact current-capital technology gap decomposes into <strong>${signed(f.withinFirmPercent,3)}%</strong> from differences in individual firm knowledge, plus <strong>${signed(f.reallocationPercent,3)}%</strong> from different capital weights, plus <strong>${signed(f.populationPercent,3)}%</strong> from unmatched firm populations, totaling <strong>${signed(f.totalPercent,3)}%</strong>. This is a single-seed decomposition, distinct from the median above. ${f.firmsWithLessKnowledge} of ${f.commonFirms} common firm IDs end with less knowledge. Scenario-only IDs: ${f.scenarioOnlyFirms}; reference-only IDs: ${f.referenceOnlyFirms}. The unmatched contribution combines population differences across the two paths; it is not a causal estimate of exit.</p>`:''}<p>Full paths may also change payroll, employment, demand, investment, debt and later research. Their empirical sizes are not identified here. A direct positive learning term does not certify a positive GDP effect.</p></details>
 <details><summary>Inspect the GDP accounting difference${paired.some(x=>x.output<0)?' for a negative GDP path':''}</summary><p>Seed ${seed}: reference GDP ${fmt(b.output,3)} → scenario GDP ${fmt(s.output,3)}. This is an accounting decomposition, not a causal identification.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Final-period component</th><th>Scenario − reference, model units</th></tr></thead><tbody>${contributions.map(x=>`<tr><td>${x.label}</td><td>${signed(x.value,4)}</td></tr>`).join('')}<tr><td>Total GDP difference</td><td>${signed(s.output-b.output,4)}</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Median feedback differences across seeds: employment ${signed(d.feedback.employment.median,2)} pp; wages ${signed(d.feedback.wages.median,2)} units; research payroll ${signed(d.feedback.rd.median,3)} units. These channels describe this simulation; they are not evidence that real-world diffusion reduces GDP.</p></details>`;
}
function renderSensitivityResult(){
 const host=$('#sensitivity-results');if(!sensitivityResult){host.innerHTML='<p class="small muted">Check whether the result survives alternative wage, spending, market-selection, fiscal and public-knowledge assumptions. Every specification is retained.</p>';return;}
 const s=sensitivityResult.result;host.innerHTML=`<p class="small">${s.simulations} runs · ${s.seedsPerSpecification} paired seeds per specification · settings captured when this check began. Later edits require another check.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Illustrative specification</th><th>GDP gap %</th><th>Real GDP per capita growth gap · pp</th><th>Current-weight technology gap %</th><th>Flagged paths</th></tr></thead><tbody>${s.rows.map(x=>`<tr><td>${esc(x.label)}</td><td>${signed(x.gdp,2)}</td><td>${signed(x.realGDPPerCapitaGrowth,2)}</td><td>${signed(x.technology,2)}</td><td>${x.flaggedPaths}/${x.simulations}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div><p class="small">${esc(s.note)} Flagged paths reach employment below 50% or debt above 300% of nominal GDP; these cutoffs are diagnostic assumptions. A stable sign across these probes is not empirical validation.</p>`;
}
function empiricalAuditSection(){
 const p=profile(),rows=buildParameterAudit(p),old=diffusionAudit.previousVersion,now=diffusionAudit.updatedVersion;
 return `<section class="panel content-panel"><div class="eyebrow">COMPLETE PARAMETER AUDIT · ENGINE ${MODEL_VERSION}</div><h2>What can be corrected, and what cannot be identified?</h2><p>All ${PARAMETERS.length} parameters are audited below: ${editableParameters.length} editable controls and ${PARAMETERS.length-editableParameters.length} opening source values. Observed inputs, proxy mappings, numerical choices and behavioral coefficients answer different questions. <strong>No causal innovation or diffusion coefficient has been estimated for this ABM.</strong> An unestimated assumption is not automatically false, but it cannot be presented as a validated fact.</p><ul><li><strong>Corrected:</strong> total business GERD is no longer treated as 100% payroll. Eurostat labor-cost shares are used for 24 presets; France, Ireland, the Netherlands and EU27 retain a visible 60% assumption. Only funded payroll affects research hours and innovation. Non-labor GERD remains an unfinanced benchmark outside this simplified production account. Public business grants use a proportional labor allocation, also a proxy.</li><li><strong>Removed from the default baseline:</strong> universal debt-driven spending cuts and a public-R&D frontier multiplier. Their response coefficients were not estimated, and the same debt target was imposed across country presets. Both remain explicit optional experiments; a zero default does not estimate their true effects.</li><li><strong>Clarified:</strong> per-capita GDP growth and technology with current capital weights are separate metrics. Their results are not silently forced positive. The comparison audit lists every initial-setting difference and checks the actual adoption rate against the actual reference.</li><li><strong>Tested:</strong> same-state faster adoption cannot reduce knowledge. Pure learning is tested across firms and time; exact weight and GDP decompositions explain the displayed outcomes. The wage, spending, financial and public-knowledge responses remain unestimated and can be stress-tested.</li><li><strong>Still not established:</strong> full macroeconomic realism, causal policy multipliers and forecasting accuracy. Poor baseline paths remain in the results. Source-year alignment and accounting PASS do not establish these properties.</li></ul>
 ${old?`<div class="evidence-callout">Archived 1.2 diffusion audit: ${old.counts.negativeGDP}/${old.counts.pairs} standard diffusion pairs ended with lower GDP, but ${old.counts.negativeTechFinal}/${old.counts.pairs} with lower current-weight technology. No negative technology gap appeared anywhere in these standard paths. This archived protocol has a different mechanism set and seed count; it is not a matched before/after accuracy comparison. Use the live comparison audit to inspect custom configurations.${now?` With the 1.3 mechanisms enabled and a different seed count, ${now.counts.negativeGDP}/${now.counts.pairs} current pairs end with lower GDP and ${now.counts.negativeTechFinal}/${now.counts.pairs} with lower current-weight technology.`:''}</div>`:''}
 <p>Firm-level research supports uneven catch-up, absorption constraints and reallocation. It does not imply that every annual GDP response must be positive, or identify a universal fraction of knowledge adopted. The model’s negative GDP paths are conditional outcomes of its assumed demand and finance rules, not a finding about the real economy.</p>${MECHANISM_SOURCES.map(x=>`<a class="source-link" href="${esc(x.url)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${esc(x.title)} ↗</a>`).join('')}
 <details class="audit-details"><summary>Inspect all ${PARAMETERS.length} parameters for ${esc(p.name)}</summary><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Input basis</th><th>What is verified</th><th>Evidence still required</th></tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map(x=>`<tr><td>${esc(x.label)}<br>${fmt(x.preset,4)} ${esc(x.unit)}</td><td>${esc(x.inputBasis)}<br>${esc(x.source)}</td><td>${esc(x.validation)}</td><td>${esc(x.requiredEvidence)}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div></details><a class="source-link" href="./data/parameter_audit.json" download>Complete parameter audit ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/diffusion_audit.json" download>Diffusion reproduction and tests ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/assumption_sensitivity.json" download>Predeclared sensitivity results ↓</a></section>`;
}

function renderFirmEvent(event){
 const restructuring=event.type==='restructuring';
 const label=event.type==='entry'?'NEW':restructuring?'RESTRUCTURE':'EXIT';
 const asset=restructuring?'Debt reduced; firm continues':`Technology ${fmt(event.a,3)}`;
 const detail=restructuring?(event.numericalFloor?'Minimum-firm numerical constraint':'Fixed-population default')
   :event.type==='exit'?(event.financialDefault?'Financial default':`Exit probability ${fmt(event.probability*100,1)}%`):`Equity ${fmt(event.equityFunding,2)}`;
 return `<div><span class="event-kind ${event.type}">${label}</span><span>Firm ${event.id+1}</span><span>${asset}</span><span>${detail}</span></div>`;
}

function renderRenewal(){
 const host=$('#renewal-panel');if(!host||!result)return;
 const r=result,t=selectedTime,run=r.first.scenario,s=run.rows[t],b=r.first.baseline.rows[t],previous=run.rows[Math.max(0,t-1)];
 const events=run.events.filter(x=>x.t===t);
 host.innerHTML=`<div class="section-header"><div><div class="eyebrow">FIRM RENEWAL</div><h2>A changing population, with traceable learning.</h2></div><button class="button secondary" id="population-chart">Chart active firms ↗</button></div>
 <p class="small muted">Displayed scenario seed ${run.seed} · period ${t}. The network timeline also changes this panel. Counts are for this seed; headline effects above summarize paired seeds.</p>
 <div class="renewal-stats"><div><span>Active firms</span><strong>${s.firmCount}</strong><small>Reference ${b.firmCount} · initially ${r.params.firms}</small></div><div><span>New firms / exits</span><strong>${s.entries} / ${s.exits}</strong><small>This step · cumulative ${s.cumulativeEntries} / ${s.cumulativeExits}</small></div><div><span>Foreign adopters</span><strong>${s.foreignAdopters}</strong><small>Positive learning events this step</small></div><div><span>Dormant capital</span><strong>${fmt(s.dormantCapital,2)}</strong><small>Unsold liquidation assets · model units</small></div></div>
 <p>At the same size and arrears, less productive technology increases relative exit risk. Financial default can also trigger exit. Startups use household equity to buy existing capital; liquidation settles creditor recoveries and returns residual cash to owners. No one-for-one replacement is imposed.</p>
 <div class="renewal-legend"><span><i style="background:#4f85b7"></i>New firm</span><span><i style="background:#9462ab"></i>Foreign learning</span><span><i style="background:#c3a35a"></i>Own innovation</span></div>
 <details><summary>Inspect turnover, funding and events</summary><p>Startup equity funded this step: ${fmt(s.startupFunding,3)} units. Gross loan write-offs: ${fmt(s.loanWriteoffs,3)}; liquidation recoveries: ${fmt(s.loanRecoveries,3)}. Secondhand capital transferred: ${fmt(s.capitalTransfers,3)} units; these transfers are excluded from GDP and new investment. Employment in the main chart measures labor used during the period; exits happen at its close.</p><p>Startup opportunities: ${s.entryAttempts}, successful: ${s.entries}, unfunded or without sufficient assets: ${s.unfundedEntries}, blocked at the population ceiling: ${s.capBlockedEntries}. Exits blocked by the two-firm numerical floor: ${s.floorBlockedExits}. ${t?`Population reconciliation: ${previous.firmCount} + ${s.entries} − ${s.exits} = ${s.firmCount}.`:''}</p>${events.length?`<div class="event-list">${events.slice(0,10).map(renderFirmEvent).join('')}</div>${events.length>10?`<p class="small">${events.length-10} more events are retained in the full experiment export.</p>`:''}`:'<p class="small muted">No entry, exit or restructuring at this displayed period.</p>'}</details>`;
}

function renewalEvidence(){
 const a=turnoverAudit;if(!a.currentEngineRuns)return '';
 return `<section class="panel content-panel"><div class="eyebrow">ENTRY, EXIT & FOREIGN ADOPTION · ORIGINAL-CHANNEL STUDY</div><h2>Measured starting points. Explicit firm-level assumptions.</h2><p>The 28 European presets use matched 2023 Eurostat enterprise birth/death rates, grouped firm-size dispersion, startup size and descriptive size/exit relationships. PWT 11.0 provides 2023 PPP TFP levels for home, the USA and China and the inputs to the rest-of-world average. Firm failure selection, the donor premium and adoption speeds remain assumptions.</p><p>Foreign ratios use <code>ctfp</code>, which supports cross-country comparisons. Historical donor growth uses the separate <code>rtfpna</code> series over 2013–2023. Its use as a future constant is an extrapolation. EU27 has no observed PWT aggregate TFP: the home denominator is an explicitly labeled output-weighted national-mean proxy.</p><p>Permanent firm IDs prevent re-indexing after an exit from assigning another firm's history or random stream. Newborn firms cannot appear in a peer's pre-birth information. Demand targets now reflect productive capacity rather than treating differently sized firms as identical. Funded asset auctions, unresolved liquidation estates, creditor recoveries and household-financed entry preserve the bank, sector, capital and goods identities.</p>
 <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Predeclared channel combination</th><th>Runs</th><th>Employment below 50%</th><th>Debt above 300% GDP</th><th>Population floor / cap</th></tr></thead><tbody>${a.factorial.map(x=>`<tr><td>${esc({legacy_fixed:'Both new channels off',turnover_only:'Entry/exit only',foreign_only:'Foreign adoption only',both:'Both enabled'}[x.mode])}</td><td>${x.runs}</td><td>${x.employmentBelow50}</td><td>${x.debtAbove300GDP}</td><td>${x.floorBound} / ${x.capBound}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
 <p class="small">Same 28 presets × five seeds × four combinations, 40 steps. A path can trigger several flags. These thresholds are diagnostic assumptions. Every outcome is retained. This comparison tests mechanisms and plausibility; it does not measure forecast accuracy or select parameters.</p><p>This original-channel study tested ${a.currentEngineRuns} paths with the four new channels off plus ${a.frozenEngineRuns} separately counted frozen-engine paths. Largest relative financial/goods residual: ${a.maxAccountingResidual.toExponential(2)}. Largest capital-stock residual: ${a.maxCapitalResidual.toExponential(2)}. ${a.pairedSummary.negativeGDP}/${a.pairedSummary.pairs} both-on paths have lower final GDP than both-off; negative outcomes are retained.</p>
 <p>Across ${sizeDiagnostics.initialStates} opening states, the mean absolute discrepancy from observed employment exit shares is ${fmt(sizeDiagnostics.meanAbsoluteMomentDiscrepancyPP?.mapped,2)} percentage points with the size mapping, versus ${fmt(sizeDiagnostics.meanAbsoluteMomentDiscrepancyPP?.earlier,2)} with the earlier mapping. This moment was inspected during development; the comparison is in-sample and does not measure forecast accuracy. The remaining gap is retained.</p><a class="source-link" href="./data/size_mapping_diagnostics.json" download>Opening size / exit-moment diagnostics ↓</a><div class="check-list">${a.checks.map(x=>`<div class="check-item">${esc(x)}</div>`).join('')}</div>
 <p>Enterprise rates describe a broad business economy, including non-employers. They are proxy targets for synthetic firms. Applying a single year repeatedly over a long horizon is not empirically validated. Selection, startup financing, liquidation prices and the foreign leader premium remain unestimated; extreme country observations and binding population bounds require particular care.</p>
 <a class="source-link" href="./data/turnover_validation.json" download>Executed firm-dynamics validation ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/turnover_experiments.csv" download>Every factorial outcome · CSV ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/business_demography_observations.csv" download>Eurostat enterprise observations + flags ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/enterprise_size_observations.csv" download>Size-class counts, jobs and deaths ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/pwt_foreign_observations.csv" download>PWT productivity observations ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/pwt_rest_of_world_weights.csv" download>Rest-of-world members and weights ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/foreign_frontiers.json" download>Foreign-source mappings ↓</a></section>`;
}

function laborEvidence(p){
 const a=employmentAudit;
 const labels={previous:'Previous engine',labor_and_settlement:'Labor matching and procurement settlement',current:'Current engine with rest-of-world donor'};
 const rate=p.params.employmentRate, separation=p.params.employmentSeparation, finding=p.params.jobFindingRate;
 return `<section class="panel content-panel"><div class="eyebrow">ANNUAL LABOR MARKET</div><h2>Employment, hiring and settlement</h2>
 <p>Each step is one model year. ${esc(p.name)} opens at ${fmt(rate*p.params.initialParticipationRate/100,1)}% employment in the population (${esc(p.labourAge??'15–64')}), with ${fmt(p.params.initialParticipationRate,1)}% participating in the labour force. Within that labour force, ${fmt(rate,1)}% are employed and ${fmt(100-rate,1)}% unemployed. The preset uses ${fmt(separation,2)}% annual separation and ${fmt(finding,2)}% annual job finding. ${['USA','CHN'].includes(p.code)?'Job separation and finding are explicit missing-data assumptions for this preset.':'Eurostat transitions condition on an employed or unemployed endpoint and use broader ages15–74as an explicit proxy.'} The source table labels EU27 fallback rates where a national pair is incomplete.</p>
 <p>Firms adjust hours after matching workers. Funded jobs and unemployment reconcile to the labor force, and public borrowing pays for delivered purchases. The prior engine could reach 100% employment and could borrow for orders that remained unfilled.</p>
 <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>Paths</th><th>Reached 100% employment</th><th>Peak employment</th><th>Below 50% employment</th><th>Debt above 300% of GDP</th></tr></thead><tbody>${a.comparison.map(x=>`<tr><td>${esc(labels[x.mode])}</td><td>${x.runs}</td><td>${x.hit100}</td><td>${fmt(x.maxEmployment,2)}%</td><td>${x.employmentBelow50}</td><td>${x.debtAbove300GDP}</td></tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>
 <p class="small">28 presets, five shared seeds and 40 annual steps per path. These screens measure model behavior. Long-run forecast accuracy still needs independent validation.</p>
 <a class="source-link" href="./data/employment_audit.json" download>Executed employment and settlement checks ↓</a><a class="source-link" href="./data/labour_market_transitions.csv" download>Eurostat annual transitions and flags ↓</a></section>`;
}
